Recommend a pipeline grouping pass over the current persisted funder dataset, after declaration loading has established final funder IDs. The existing loader updates only declarations in a run and retains others. Grouping solely the new artifact would miss cross-run alias bridges and retained legacy identities.

Concrete domain modules:

```rust
// domain/models/funder_grouping.rs
struct GroupingFunder {
    id: FunderId,
    name: RecordedFunderName,
    aliases: Vec<RecordedFunderName>,
}

struct NormalizedGroupingName(String);
struct ReviewedFamilyKey(String);

enum GroupingEvidence {
    SharedAlias {
        normalized: NormalizedGroupingName,
        left_name: RecordedFunderName,
        right_name: RecordedFunderName,
    },
    ReviewedFamily {
        family: ReviewedFamilyKey,
        left_name: RecordedFunderName,
        right_name: RecordedFunderName,
    },
}

struct GroupingEdge {
    left: FunderId,
    right: FunderId,
    evidence: GroupingEvidence,
}

struct FunderGroupingProjection {
    memberships: Vec<FunderGroupMembership>,
    edges: Vec<GroupingEdge>,
    policy_version: GroupingPolicyVersion,
}
```

A pure `derive_funder_groups(funders, reviewed_families)` function owns normalization, edge generation and connected components. It emits singleton memberships too. Order edge endpoints canonically; sort and deduplicate results. Use the minimum UUID in each component as its representative to preserve PR behavior. Document that this ID changes if membership changes, so it is a representative, not a permanent family identity.

Preserve every direct edge with its original spellings and matching basis. A transitive connection has no direct evidence of its own. Keep reviewed family definitions in a versioned pipeline resource, parsed into typed definitions. Remove business data seeds from the new schema migration.

The durable schema needs membership and provenance tables plus projection metadata:

- Membership has `funder_id` as primary key and an indexed `group_id`.
- Alias edges store endpoint IDs, normalized alias and both source spellings.
- Reviewed family edges store endpoint IDs, family key and both source names.
- Metadata records the policy version and an input fingerprint.

Separate edge tables fit the current details API and avoid loosely typed nullable evidence columns. Rust has the canonical evidence enum; persistence uses exhaustive matches.

For clean service ownership, expose a transaction/session port:

```rust
trait DeclarationProjectionSession {
    async fn apply_load(&mut self, load: &DeclarationLoad)
        -> Result<LoadDisposition, Error>;
    async fn read_grouping_funders(&mut self)
        -> Result<Vec<GroupingFunder>, Error>;
    async fn replace_groups(&mut self, groups: &FunderGroupingProjection)
        -> Result<(), Error>;
    async fn commit(self) -> Result<(), Error>;
}
```

The loader service opens the session, applies the existing load, reads the final current dataset, calls the pure grouping function, replaces the projection and commits. The PostgreSQL adapter owns SQL and transaction mechanics. Grouping rules stay in domain code.

A smaller implementation can have the current atomic loader adapter call that same pure domain function after cleanup and before commit. This preserves atomicity with less refactoring, but the separate session port makes pipeline orchestration explicit.

The refresh/backfill command uses the same session and domain function without applying declaration artifacts. It acquires the existing `exposed.declaration-loader` advisory transaction lock before reading source rows. Full replacement naturally handles component splits after alias removal. Readers see the old complete projection or the new complete projection. Failure rolls back both loaded data and grouping changes.

Do not put grouping refresh solely after the current exact-retry early return. An already imported run may need a refreshed grouping policy. Either perform the grouping check before returning `AlreadyLoaded`, or make policy refresh an explicit required deployment command. The first approach gives stronger convergence.

Queries become ordinary compiled SQLx reads and joins. Search joins scored funders to stored membership. Details fetches members sharing the requested funder's group and reads stored evidence. No recursive query or normalization belongs in request queries.

Computing groups during artifact resolution offers reproducible grouping artifacts, but the resolution run does not contain the complete persisted dataset, and database UUIDs are assigned during loading. It also risks confusing search grouping with legal identity resolution. The better fit is a distinct pipeline projection pass at publication time, with an optional exported grouping artifact for audit.

Keep every applied migration immutable. Add a new reversible migration pair that drops the grouping views and creates projection tables. Its down migration removes only that pair's tables and restores the prior view definitions. Run the pipeline backfill separately before exposing the new readers; an empty projection must never become the serving state. The safest rollout keeps old readers active until the backfill commits, then deploys the stored-projection readers.

Verification should exercise transitive bridges across separate loads, singleton coverage, component splitting after edge deletion, reviewed family provenance, retained pre-pipeline rows, policy refresh on exact retry, and rollback of a failed replacement. Existing tests that insert aliases and immediately query views must explicitly run the grouping pass.
